  "worked_solution": "First, we recall the following definitions:\n\n- The global minimum of a function is the smallest value that the function attains on its domain. There can only be (at most) one global minimum.\n\n- A global minimum point of a function is a point $(x,y)$ where a function reaches a global minimum. There can be zero, one, or many global minimum points.\n\nFor our function, the minimum value reached by the function is $f(x)=-4.$ Therefore, the global minimum is $-4.$ The function reaches this value at $x=2$, so the global minimum point is $(2,-4).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14252895/assets/9b5d10eb2ebc424c4096e25dd26af905e9915bfcbeb777471b559280af6fd7ef.png)",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
